@*
 * FILE ROLE: Shared layout template — the page shell used by all views that
 *   opt into this layout. Demonstrates how ASP.NET Core MVC composes a full
 *   HTML page from a shared skeleton and per-view body content, so each view
 *   only writes the content that is unique to that page.
 *
 * SECTIONS IN THIS FILE:
 *   SECTION 15: Layout — shared page shell
 *               (sub-sections: 15a @RenderBody, 15b ViewData["Title"],
 *                              15c @RenderSection for optional page-level slots)
 *
 * DISCOVERY: the Razor view engine finds this file at:
 *   Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml
 *   Referenced from a view via:  @{ Layout = "_Layout"; }
 *   or globally from:            Views/_ViewStart.cshtml  →  Layout = "_Layout"
 *
 *   The leading underscore (_) is a convention meaning "not a standalone view";
 *   the framework will not route a request directly to a file starting with _.
 *
 * COVERED IN DETAIL LATER → 04. Layouts Sections & Partial Views
 *@

@*
 * --- 15a. LAYOUT COMPOSITION — @RenderBody() ---
 *
 * A layout file is a Razor template that contains the HTML shell and exactly
 * one call to @RenderBody(). At render time the framework:
 *   1. Renders the child view (e.g. Views/Home/Index.cshtml) to an HTML string
 *   2. Replaces @RenderBody() in the layout with that HTML string
 *   3. Returns the combined result as the HTTP response
 *
 * This eliminates duplication of <html>, <head>, <nav>, and <footer> across
 * every view in the application. Changing the site navigation once in
 * _Layout.cshtml updates all pages simultaneously.
 *
 * LAYOUT NESTING:
 *   A layout can itself declare a parent layout. This enables admin vs public
 *   sections that share a base shell but differ in navigation structure or
 *   sidebar panels:
 *     _AdminLayout.cshtml sets Layout = "_Layout";   // nests inside this file
 *
 * DRY CONTENT IN LAYOUTS — everything that appears on every page goes here:
 *   <meta> tags, <link rel="stylesheet">, Google Analytics, site navigation,
 *   breadcrumbs, global footer, cookie consent banners.
 *@

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />

    @*
     * --- 15b. ViewData["Title"] — DATA FROM VIEW TO LAYOUT ---
     *
     * ViewData is a ViewDataDictionary (string → object?) shared between the
     * controller, the child view, and the layout. The child view writes:
     *   @{ ViewData["Title"] = "Product Details"; }
     * The layout reads it back with @ViewData["Title"].
     *
     * Why not use a field or property? Layout and child views are separate
     * compiled Razor classes that share ViewData as a communication channel
     * (they do not share a common base class instance).
     *
     * Alternatives for passing data from a view to its layout:
     *   ViewBag.Title       dynamic wrapper over ViewData (convenient but
     *                       not refactor-safe — no IntelliSense on keys)
     *   @RenderSection      for larger HTML blocks, not scalar values
     *   IViewContextAware   services that can access ViewContext directly
     *
     * Null-coalescing (??) provides a fallback title when the child view
     * does not set ViewData["Title"] (e.g. error pages, 404 pages).
     *
     * COVERED IN DETAIL LATER → 12. TempData ViewData & ViewBag
     *@
    <title>@(ViewData["Title"] ?? "MVC Introduction App")</title>  @* ?? = fallback when key not set *@
</head>
<body>

    <header>
        <nav style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; background: #1a1a2e; color: #eee;">
            <a href="/" style="color: #e0e0e0; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;">
                ASP.NET Core MVC — Introduction
            </a>
        </nav>
    </header>

    @*
     * --- 15c. @RenderBody() and @RenderSection — INJECTION POINTS ---
     *
     * @RenderBody() marks the point where the child view's HTML is injected.
     * There is exactly one @RenderBody() per layout file.
     *
     * @RenderSection(name, required) provides named injection slots for
     * optional or required blocks of HTML from the child view:
     *
     *   In the layout (this file):
     *     @RenderSection("scripts", required: false)
     *
     *   In the child view:
     *     @section scripts {
     *         <script src="~/js/page-specific.js"></script>
     *     }
     *
     *   required: false  the layout renders fine if no child defines the section
     *   required: true   an exception is thrown if the child omits the section
     *
     * COMMON NAMED SECTIONS:
     *   scripts    page-specific JavaScript loaded at the bottom of <body>
     *   styles     page-specific CSS loaded inside <head>
     *   sidebar    per-page sidebar content in a two-column layout template
     *   breadcrumb per-page breadcrumb trail injected into the header area
     *
     * COVERED IN DETAIL LATER → 04. Layouts Sections & Partial Views
     *@
    <main style="padding: 1rem;">
        @RenderBody()   @* child view HTML is injected here at render time *@
    </main>

    <footer style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; background: #f0f0f0; color: #555; font-size: 0.85rem;">
        <p>05. ASP.NET Core MVC — Introduction to MVC Pattern</p>
    </footer>

    @RenderSection("scripts", required: false)  @* optional: page-specific scripts at body end *@

</body>
</html>
